      "thinking": "Let me analyze this certificate fragment.\n\nCertificate details:\n- Certificate No.: R49/2013-A-SK1-26.08\n- Scheme: A\n- OIML Member State: Slovakia\n- Issuing Authority: Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM), address: Geologická 9966/1, 821 06 Bratislava-Podunajské Biskupice, Slovakia; PCB address: Hviedzoslavova 31, 974 01 Banská Bystrica, Slovakia; Person responsible: Dušan Šmigura, Director of PCB\n- Date issued: 31st August 2026 (from revision history and signature date)\n- Applicant/Manufacturer: Hangzhou Laison Technology Co., Ltd., No. 525 Xixi Road, 310000 Hangzhou, Zhejiang, China\n- Certified type: Water meter type LXCZ (family DN15, DN20, DN25)\n- Module designation: Ultrasonic water meters with electronic indication device\n- Recommendation: OIML R 49, Edition 2013\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- Page total: 10\n\nTest reports:\n- OIML Type Evaluation Report No. 2026/ER017/SK1, dated 31st August 2026, 16 pages, role: type_evaluation_report (evaluation report)\n- Technical documentation file \"Technical documentation file Hangzhou Liaison_LXCZ_00_plastic\", dated 31st August 2026, 88 pages, role: documentation_file\n\nRevision history:\n- Revision 0, 31 August 2026, Certificate first issued\n\nPage total: 10 (from \"Page 1 of 10\")\n\nThe summary says the certification body address is \"Hviedzoslavova 31, 974 01 Banská Bystrica\" — labeled as \"Product Certification Body\". The main address is Geologická 9966/1, 821 06 Bratislava-Podunajské Biskupice, Slovakia.\n\noiml_issuer_id: not present explicitly. Skip.\n\nCertified type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" — actually the category list in schema includes \"Water meter\"? The examples list: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\", ... — I can use \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: [\"LXCZ\"]\n- module_designation: \"Ultrasonic water meters with electronic indication device\"\n- description: from page 3 — measures, memorizes, displays volume of water passing through measurement transducers; residential compact ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication, ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time principle...\n\nModel family: DN15, DN20, DN25 variants. The table on page 4:\n\n| Parameter | Unit | DN15 | DN20 | DN25 |\n- Material of body: plastic, plastic, plastic (type-level)\n- Permanent flowrate Q3: m³/h: 2.5, 4.0, 6.3\n- Minimum flowrate Q1: m³/h: 0.00625, 0.010, 0.01575\n- Transitional flowrate Q2: m³/h: 0.010, 0.016, 0.0252\n- Overload flowrate Q4: m³/h: 3.125, 5.0, 7.875\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 400, 400, 400 (constant → could be type_level)\n- Ratio Q2/Q1: 1.6, 1.6, 1.6 (constant)\n- Connection thread: G¾ B, G1 B, G1 ¼ B (string values, model-varying)\n- Construction length L: mm: 165, 190, 225\n\nCommon parameters (all sizes) → type_level:\n- installation_orientation: \"H/V\"\n- water_temperature_range: 0.1–50 °C (T50 class) — value {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure (MAP): 16 bar\n- pressure_loss: 0.63 bar (class Δp 63)\n- ingress_protection: \"IP68\"\n- max_permissible_error_upper_range: ±2% (0.1–30°C); ±3% (30–50°C) — could record as config_level (temperature range axis) or just strings. Let me do config_level with axis \"temperature_range\": \n  - condition \"Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4, 0.1–30 °C\": value 2, unit \"%\"\n  - condition \"Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4, 30–50 °C\": value 3, unit \"%\"\n  - Actually the lower range: ±5% for Q1 ≤ Q < Q2.\n  \n  Better: config_level entries:\n  - attribute: maximum_permissible_error_upper_range, axis: water_temperature, values: [{condition: \"0.1–30 °C\", value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"30–50 °C\", value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\"}]\n  - attribute: maximum_permissible_error_lower_range, value 5%? Or type_level: maximum_permissible_error_lower_range: {value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\"}. Hmm, with ± sign. Value 5, unit \"%\". Sign is magnitude — MPE is ±2%. I could record value as -? No, magnitude with note. I'll keep value: 2, unit: \"%\" for upper etc. Actually \"±2%\" — could store as value: \"±2\"? Rules say numbers, not strings. The magnitude is 2%. I'll store value: 2 and attribute name includes maximum_permissible_error — the ± is implied by MPE. Fine.\n\n- scale_range_normal_precision: 0.001 m³\n- scale_range_high_precision: 0.001 L\n- calculator_capacity_normal_mode: 999999.999 m³\n- calculator_capacity_testing_mode: 999999.999 L\n- accuracy_class: 2 (dimensionless)\n\nModel family models:\n- model_id: \"DN15\", \"DN20\", \"DN25\"\n\nmodel_level characteristics:\n- permanent_flowrate_q3: m³/h — DN15: 2.5, DN20: 4.0, DN25: 6.3\n- minimum_flowrate_q1: m³/h — 0.00625, 0.010, 0.01575\n- transitional_flowrate_q2: m³/h — 0.010, 0.016, 0.0252\n- overload_flowrate_q4: m³/h — 3.125, 5.0, 7.875\n- connection_thread: G¾B, G1B, G1¼B (string values)\n- construction_length: mm — 165, 190, 225\n\nConstant across models (ratio Q3/Q1 = 400, ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6, material plastic) → type_level:\n- ratio_q3_q1: 400\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6\n- body_material: \"plastic\"\n\nMatrix table: also emit matrix_tables for the technical data table.\n\nSoftware:\n- Software version LS456802, checksum 759d413A, remarks \"Plastic body\". Could be in components or characteristics type_level: software_version: \"LS456802\", software_checksum: \"759d413A\". Or components entry with role \"software\"? The schema has components for sub-assemblies. Software is arguably a component. I could add a components entry: role: \"Legal software\", type_designations: [\"LS456802\"], characteristics: {checksum: \"759d413A\", remarks: \"Plastic body\"}. Hmm, characteristics{} is free-form object. I'll include it maybe as type_level characteristics instead: legal_software_version: \"LS456802\", legal_software_checksum: \"759d413A\".\n\nBattery: Umax: 3.6 V; 19 Ah; 10-year lifetime. Could add component: battery. Components section says include only if cert names sub-assemblies. Essential parts: water meter body, flow sensor, calculator and indication device. These are described but without type designations. I might add components:\n- role: \"calculator_and_indication_device\" with characteristics battery...\nHmm. Keep it reasonable: I could add components for calculator/indication with battery characteristics. Let me add:\n\ncomponents[]:\n- {role: \"calculator_and_indication_device\", type_designations: [], characteristics: {display: \"Electronic scrolling LCD with 9 digits\", indication_range: 999999.999, ...}} — hmm, the characteristics in components is free-form. But the schema example shows characteristics{} object. I'll include a modest entry.\n\nActually maybe better to keep characteristics in the main characteristics block and add components with role and key specs:\n\n- role: \"flow_sensor\", characteristics: {principle: \"ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time\"}\n- role: \"calculator_and_indication_device\", characteristics: {display: \"electronic scrolling LCD, 9 digits\", battery_voltage_max: 3.6 V, battery_capacity: 19 Ah, battery_lifetime: 10 years, battery_type: \"lithium, replaceable\"}\n- role: \"legal_software\", type_designations: [\"LS456802\"], characteristics: {checksum: \"759d413A\", remarks: \"Plastic body\"}\n\nHmm, but don't over-engineer. The instructions say components for sub-assemblies named. These are named (\"Water meter body\", \"Flow sensor\", \"Calculator and indication device\"). I'll include them briefly.\n\nNon-essential parts: filter, control valve, strainer, wireless interfaces (IR, Bluetooth, WM-Bus, LoRa, LoRaWAN, NB-IoT Standard, GPRS, Cat.1, Sigfox). Could note in description.\n\nFootnotes: none present.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013, amendment null, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes: [2].\n\nCertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-26.08\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: not present\n- page_total: 10\n- member_state: \"Slovakia\"\n- date_issued: \"2026-08-31\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\"\n- address_lines: [\"Geologická 9966/1\", \"821 06 Bratislava-Podunajské Biskupice\", \"Slovakia\"] — and PCB address Hviedzoslavova 31, 974 01 Banská Bystrica. Hmm, which is the authority address? The summary lists both. I'll include main address in address_lines and note the PCB address... There's no field for it. Maybe include both lines? The label \"Product Certification Body: Hviedzoslavova 31, 974 01 Banská Bystrica, Slovakia\". I'll add as extra address line with label? That could confuse. I'll include main address lines only, but person_responsible \"Dušan Šmigura\", person_title \"Director of PCB\". Actually maybe include the PCB address in address_lines too since it's part of the authority's locations. I'll include both: \n\naddress_lines: [\"Geologická 9966/1\", \"821 06 Bratislava-Podunajské Biskupice\", \"Slovakia\", \"Product Certification Body: Hviedzoslavova 31\", \"974 01 Banská Bystrica\", \"Slovakia\"]\n\nHmm, that's messy. Alternatively just the main address. I think including the PCB address is informative; keep as separate lines. I'll do:\n\naddress_lines: [\"Geologická 9966/1, 821 06 Bratislava-Podunajské Biskupice\", \"Slovakia\"], person_responsible: \"Dušan Šmigura\", person_title: \"Director of PCB\".\n\nActually, I'll include the PCB address line too since it was explicitly stated. Let me format:\n\naddress_lines: [\n  \"Geologická 9966/1, 821 06 Bratislava-Podunajské Biskupice, Slovakia\",\n  \"Product Certification Body: Hviedzoslavova 31, 974 01 Banská Bystrica, Slovakia\"\n]\n\nApplicants: [{name: \"Hangzhou Laison Technology Co., Ltd.\", address_lines: [\"No. 525 Xixi Road\", \"310000 Hangzhou\", \"Zhejiang\", \"China\"]}]\n\nManufacturers: same as applicant (labeled \"Applicant / Manufacturer\"). Include manufacturers entry identical.\n\nTest reports:\n- {id: \"2026/ER017/SK1\", date: \"2026-08-31\", pages: 16, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"Technical documentation file Hangzhou Liaison_LXCZ_00_plastic\", date: \"2026-08-31\", pages: 88, role: \"documentation_file\"}\n\nRevision history: [{revision: \"0\", date: \"2026-08-31\", changes: \"Certificate first issued\"}]\n\nCertified type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"LXCZ\"]\n- module_designation: \"Ultrasonic water meters with electronic indication device\"\n- description: long text summarizing.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"LXCZ\" (type name is family covering DN15/20/25)\n- models: [{model_id: \"DN15\"}, {model_id: \"DN20\"}, {model_id: \"DN25\"}]\n\ncharacteristics:\ntype_level:\n- installation_orientation: {value: \"H/V\", unit_symbol: null}\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"} — also note class T50. Could add temperature_class: \"T50\".\n- temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n- pressure_loss: {value: 0.63, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}; pressure_loss_class: {value: \"Δp 63\"} — Δp symbol; avoid LaTeX — \"Δp 63\" is fine (Unicode Δ allowed). Or value \"63\" with class label. I'll do pressure_loss_class: {value: \"Δp 63\"}.\n- ingress_protection: {value: \"IP68\"}\n- maximum_permissible_error_lower_range: {value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\"} — for Q1 ≤ Q < Q2. Maybe config_level with flow range conditions:\n  config_level:\n  - attribute: \"maximum_permissible_error\", axis: \"flow_range_and_temperature\", values: [\n      {condition: \"Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4, 0.1–30 °C\", value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\"},\n      {condition: \"Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4, 30–50 °C\", value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\"},\n      {condition: \"Q1 ≤ Q < Q2\", value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n    ]\n  That's cleaner. Use one config_level entry.\n- scale_range_normal_precision: {value: 0.001, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}\n- scale_range_high_precision: {value: 0.001, unit_symbol: \"L\"}\n- calculator_capacity_normal_mode: {value: 999999.999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}\n- calculator_capacity_testing_mode: {value: 999999.999, unit_symbol: \"L\"}\n- accuracy_class: {value: 2, unit_symbol: null}\n- ratio_q3_q1: {value: 400}\n- ratio_q2_q1: {value: 1.6}\n- body_material: {value: \"plastic\"}\n- measures_reverse_flow: {value: false}? It says \"not designed to measure reverse flow\". Could include reverse_flow_measurement: {value: \"not designed\"}. Hmm, keep description. Skip.\n- test_mode code: short code 37 via remote controller. Skip or note.\n\nAlso display indication range 999999,999 m³ — same as calculator capacity. Battery: lithium 3.6 V, 19 Ah, 10-year lifetime — put in components characteristics.\n\nmodel_level:\n- q3: permanent_flowrate_q3\n- q1: minimum_flowrate_q1\n- q2: transitional_flowrate_q2\n- q4: overload_flowrate_q4\n- connection_thread (string values \"G¾B\", \"G1B\", \"G1¼B\") — value strings with unit null.\n- construction_length (mm)\n\nValues with decimal conversion: 2,5 → 2.5; 0,010 → 0.01; 0,01575 → 0.01575; 0,0252 → 0.0252; 1,6 → 1.6; 0,00625 → 0.00625.\n\nMatrix table:\nmatrix_tables:\n- name: \"Technical and metrological data (LXCZ)\"\n  columns: [\"dn\", \"body_material\", \"q3\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"q2_q1_ratio\", \"connection_thread\", \"construction_length\"]\n  column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, null, null, \"mm\"]\n  rows: [\n    {dn: \"DN15\", body_material: \"plastic\", q3: 2.5, q1: 0.00625, q2: 0.01, q4: 3.125, q3_q1_ratio: 400, q2_q1_ratio: 1.6, connection_thread: \"G¾B\", construction_length: 165},\n    {dn: \"DN20\", ..., q3: 4.0, q1: 0.01, q2: 0.016, q4: 5.0, thread \"G1B\", length 190},\n    {dn: \"DN25\", q3: 6.3, q1: 0.01575, q2: 0.0252, q4: 7.875, thread \"G1¼B\", length 225}\n  ]\n\nConnection thread notation: \"G¾ B\" → \"G¾B\" or keep \"G3/4 B\". Keep as given: \"G¾ B\", \"G1 B\", \"G1 ¼ B\".\n\nComponents:\n- role: \"flow_sensor\", characteristics: {principle: \"ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time\", description: \"two reflection sheets, two ultrasonic transducers\"}\n- role: \"calculator_and_indication_device\", characteristics: {display: \"electronic scrolling LCD, 9 digits\", indication_range: ...}. Hmm — the schema for components characteristics is free-form {}. But rule 2 says every characteristic value must be structured object... that applies to `characteristics` block values. For components, I'll use structured values too for safety where numeric.\n\nLet me structure:\n\ncomponents: [\n  {\n    \"role\": \"calculator_and_indication_device\",\n    \"type_designations\": [],\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"display\": {\"value\": \"electronic scrolling LCD, 9 digits\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      \"battery\": {\"value\": \"replaceable lithium\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      \"battery_nominal_voltage_max\": {\"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n      \"battery_capacity\": {\"value\": 19, \"unit_symbol\": \"Ah\"},\n      \"battery_lifetime\": {\"value\": 10, \"unit_symbol\": \"years\"}\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"role\": \"legal_software\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"LS456802\"],\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"checksum\": {\"value\": \"759d413A\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      \"remarks\": {\"value\": \"Plastic body\", \"unit_symbol\": null}\n    }\n  }\n]\n\nUmax: 3.6 V — \"Umax\" is maximum voltage. I'll label battery_voltage_max.\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nDate format: \"31st August 2026\" → \"2026-08-31\".\n\nNow output a single JSON object. Order per schema. Don't include empty fields.\n\nFor type_level values, each needs the structured object shape. Schema says characteristics.type_level: <label>: {value, unit, footnote_markers[]}. Rule 2 shape: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. I'll use unit_symbol and unit_id (null omitted? \"Leave unit_id: null\" — include as null). I'll include unit_id: null consistently? That's verbose but safe. The example in rule 2 shows both keys. I'll include unit_symbol and unit_id: null, omit footnote_markers when empty (rule 8: omit if not present). Actually the schema shows footnote_markers[] as part of shape; including empty arrays is fine but rule 8 says omit fields not present. I'll omit empty footnote_markers.\n\nLet me also handle \"Ratio Q₃/Q₁ – 400\" as type_level ratio_q3_q1 value 400 dimensionless.\n\nAccuracy class 2 also in recommendation.\n\nCategory: \"Water meter\".\n\nPage total 10.\n\nWrite JSON now.",
